{
  "statement": "The Safetyist argues that a centralized database provides the necessary oversight to prevent catastrophic model failures, while the Accelerationist insists that such friction merely slows down necessary progress. Both rely on the fantasy that a government-run repository will function like a benign library. In reality, a centralized National Database of AI Flaws acts as a high-value honeypot, handing adversarial nation-states a target list of zero-day vulnerabilities in critical infrastructure. History shows that when you centralize intelligence on system weaknesses, you don't just enable patches; you provide a roadmap for exploitation to any actor with the resources to breach a single, massive perimeter.\n\nWe have seen this failure mode before in the CVE system, which tracks thousands of software vulnerabilities annually. Research indicates that the time-to-exploit for documented vulnerabilities often drops to under 48 hours once they appear in public databases. By forcing AI developers to report flaws to a single federal repository, we are essentially building a centralized kill-switch for the national grid, financial systems, and logistics networks. This is not a defensive asset; it is a tactical gift to every foreign intelligence agency currently looking to bypass our digital defenses. If this database is breached—and history suggests it will be—the damage will be systemic and irreversible.\n\nBeyond the security risk, this policy serves as a mechanism for regulatory capture. By requiring standardized, centralized reporting, the state allows incumbent AI giants to define what constitutes a 'flaw' while potentially burying or de-prioritizing vulnerabilities that threaten their own business models. Smaller, agile competitors will be crushed under the weight of the compliance infrastructure required to interface with this federal monolith. We are building a moat for the industry leaders, paid for by the taxpayer, under the guise of national safety. Before we hand the keys to our digital infrastructure to a federal agency, we should ask who actually benefits when the data is consolidated: the public, or the firms who stand to gain from suppressing independent, distributed security audits?",
